#: ../../userpreferences.rst:52
msgid "Sharing your data in the global address book"
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:54
msgid "Some administrators share their global address book with other Nextcloud instances (so-called *Trusted Servers*) or even with the wider world. This is helpful when two instances want to work closely together, or when people want to use Nextcloud as a virtual telephone book for others to browse. It also allows searching for contacts, creating shares, and much more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:60
msgid "You can change what personal data is shared by setting the scope of your data. Click the lock icon to open the following dropdown next to each entry:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:63
#: ../../userpreferences.rst:100
msgid "Visibility scope dropdown on a personal information field with Private, Local, Federated, and Published options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:67
msgid "If you set your data to **Private**, nobody but you will be able to see it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:69
msgid "If you set your data to **Local**, all logged-in users within your Nextcloud instance will be able to see the information, but no one outside of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:72
msgid "If you set your data to **Federated**, the trusted server(s) added by your administrator will be able to see this data, in addition to all logged-in users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../userpreferences.rst:75
msgid "If you set your data to **Published**, anyone can see your data. This is useful in some cases. Someone with a public-facing role such as marketing or sales might want to share their contact details with a wide variety of connections who might not be using Nextcloud."
msgstr ""

